-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SURFACE IMPLEMENTATION (parity port; no stubs / no placeholder / no skeleton-only)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Native WinUI 3 port of the web ToastProvider overlay: a bottom-right stack of auto-dismissing typed cards
  (success / error / info / warning). Each card carries the shared-callout leading glyph + accent (native
  stand-in for the per-type Lucide icon + 300-level border), a bold title, an optional secondary message
  (2-line clamp), an optional action, and a dismiss button. Built as a code-only ContentControl (no XAML),
  mirroring the OfflineBanner / LiveStaleDataBanner shared-surface pattern.
- 4-file surface: Toast.cs (WinUI view), Toast.Model.cs + Toast.Source.cs + Toast.ViewModel.cs (Microsoft.UI-free
  -> Compile-linked into the headless test host; the bare view stays app-build-only).
- DATA SOURCES bound (the web hooks the prompt lists), all via the P1/S8 state-holder layer, no direct HTTP/IO
  from the view:
    useToast          -> IToastController (Snapshot + Changed + Show/Dismiss) bound through ToastViewModel.
    useOptionalToast  -> ToastAccess.Optional(controller?) (returns null when no overlay is hosted).
    useToast (throw)  -> ToastAccess.Require(controller?) (throws the web message verbatim,
                         "useToast must be used within ToastProvider").
    useMutationToast  -> ToastMutationReporter (i18n-aware success(key,fallback) / error(err,key?,fallback?)
                         with the web 'toast.common.error' / "Something went wrong" defaults and the
                         err.message-as-detail coercion).
    useMotionPreference -> Components.Motion.MotionPreference.ReduceMotion (entrance/exit snap when reduced).
- DRY / single source of truth: the four tones reuse the shared CalloutVariant + CalloutVariants family
  (glyph, accent brush, assertiveness) rather than a parallel enum. Web ToastType -> CalloutVariant: success ->
  Success, error -> Danger, info -> Info, warning -> Warning. The accent COLOUR token (for the alpha-tinted
  border) is paired in ToastColors. No duplicated tone tables.
- Queue policy reproduces the web ToastProvider exactly: ids "toast-N" (web `toast-${++toastCounter}`), default
  4000 ms lifetime (web `opts.duration ?? 4000`), duration <= 0 = persistent (web `if (duration > 0)`), and a
  five-toast cap dropping the oldest from the front (web `[...prev.slice(-4), next]`). dismiss(id) filters the
  matching toast (web `prev.filter(t => t.id !== id)`).
- ALL authoritative web render branches reproduced + tested: the EMPTY overlay (collapsed, hit-transparent
  region -- a toast layer's correct resting state), the four tones, with / without secondary message, and the
  three action shapes -- navigation (HyperlinkButton "{label} ->" raising the decoupled ActionNavigationRequested
  with the in-app route, the native analogue of the web React-Router <Link>), callback (Button invoking OnClick),
  and no action -- plus the reduce-motion branch. This is a transient, client-only feedback primitive with NO
  data fetch, so -- exactly like the sibling OfflineBanner surface -- the generic loading / stale / offline
  data-states do not apply and are deliberately not fabricated.
- Auto-dismiss: each card owns a one-shot DispatcherQueueTimer (web `setTimeout(() => dismiss(id), duration)`),
  view-owned per the LiveStaleDataBanner precedent; the controller stays UI-thread-free and unit-testable.
  Clicking the dismiss button or either action requests controller.Dismiss(id) (single source of truth); the
  resulting Changed reconciles the card out with a fade/translate animation (snapped under reduce-motion).
- i18n: every literal resolves through the P1/S10 ILocalizer facade with `translation.`-prefixed keys, no
  hardcoded English in native code (English appears only as ILocalizer fallbacks):
    translation.toast.dismiss        ("Dismiss notification"  -- the web dismiss aria-label, verbatim)
    translation.toast.common.error   ("Something went wrong"  -- the web _toastHelpers default error title)
  Toast titles/messages/action labels are caller-supplied (already localized by the caller, as on the web).
- Accessibility: each card is a UI-Automation live region -- assertive for the error tone (web role="alert" /
  aria-live="assertive"), polite otherwise (web role="status") -- and is Narrator-named with title + ". " +
  message as a single atomic utterance (web aria-atomic="true"); the decorative icon/title/message are
  AccessibilityView.Raw so the region name is authoritative while the action/dismiss buttons stay operable; the
  dismiss button carries the localized "Dismiss notification" name + tooltip; the overlay exposes a Group
  automation peer + the "toast-region" automation id. Reduce-motion honoured.
- Telemetry: ToastDiagnostics emits view.opened slug=Toast once on overlay mount (P1/S11), plus PII-free
  toast.shown slug=Toast variant=<tone> / toast.dismissed slug=Toast counters -- the tone only, NEVER the
  user-supplied title/message.
- Analyzer note (TreatWarningsAsErrors + Recommended): the web context's success/error/info/warning verbs are
  provided as call-site-identical EXTENSION methods on IToastController (ToastControllerExtensions), kept off the
  interface so the reserved 'Error' name never binds a virtual/interface member (CA1716) -- no suppressions, and
  controller.Error(title, detail) still reads exactly like the web.

